<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Gödel on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/g%C3%B6del/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/g%C3%B6del/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Gödel, Turing, and the Mathematics of Horror</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-formal-foundations/</link><pubDate>Wed, 05 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-formal-foundations/</guid><description>The formal foundations of cosmic dread. Lovecraft&amp;#39;s horror resonates because it taps into something mathematically demonstrable: complete knowledge is impossible, not as humility, but as theorem.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-formal-foundations/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Gödel</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>cosmic horror</category><category>Gödel</category><category>Turing</category><category>incompleteness</category><category>halting problem</category><category>computation</category><category>mathematics</category></item><item><title>Why Artificial Superintelligence Can&amp;#39;t Escape the Void</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-asi/</link><pubDate>Wed, 05 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-asi/</guid><description>ASI is still subject to Gödel&amp;#39;s incompleteness theorems. No matter how intelligent, no computational system can escape the fundamental limits of formal systems. Even superintelligence can&amp;#39;t prove all truths.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-05-mocking-void-asi/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Gödel</media:title></media:content><category>AI</category><category>artificial intelligence</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>Gödel</category><category>incompleteness</category><category>cosmic horror</category><category>existential risk</category></item><item><title>The Mocking Void: On the Computational Incompleteness of Meaning</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-20-the-mocking-void/</link><pubDate>Tue, 20 Aug 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-20-the-mocking-void/</guid><description>Lovecraft understood that complete knowledge is madness. Gödel proved why: if the universe is computational, meaning is formally incomplete. Cosmic horror grounded in incompleteness theorems.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-20-the-mocking-void/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Gödel</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>cosmic horror</category><category>lovecraft</category><category>Gödel</category><category>incompleteness</category><category>computation</category><category>metaphysics</category></item></channel></rss>